// Class 12 English Core - Flamingo Poetry Chapter 13: Aunt Jennifer's Tigers
// Fully standardized to English Core Blueprint & AGENTS.md guidelines

export const c12EngF13HtmlOverview = "\n<div style=\"padding: 18px; color: #F8FAFC; text-align: justify; font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, sans-serif; line-height: 1.85; font-size: 15px;\">\n  <!-- Chapter Header Badge -->\n  <div style=\"background: linear-gradient(135deg, rgba(255, 82, 82, 0.18), rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.95)); border: 1.5px solid #FF5252; border-radius: 14px; padding: 22px; margin-bottom: 24px; box-shadow: 0 8px 24px rgba(0,0,0,0.35);\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 10px;\">\n      <span style=\"background: #FF5252; color: #0F172A; font-weight: 800; font-size: 12px; padding: 4px 10px; border-radius: 6px; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 1px;\">Poem 13 • Flamingo Poetry</span>\n      <span style=\"color: #94A3B8; font-size: 13px; font-weight: 600;\">Core Literature • Class 12 English</span>\n    </div>\n    <h1 style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 26px; font-weight: 800; margin: 0 0 8px 0; letter-spacing: -0.5px;\">Aunt Jennifer's Tigers</h1>\n    <p style=\"color: #FF5252; font-size: 15px; font-weight: 600; margin: 0 0 12px 0;\">By Adrienne Rich (1929–2012) • Renowned American Feminist Poet & Activist</p>\n    <p style=\"color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.6; margin: 0;\">A poignant feminist masterpiece dramatizing the debilitating constraints of patriarchal marriage, the somatic terror of domestic subjugation, and the immortal triumph of artistic rebellion through fearless needlework.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Poet Background & Context -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(30, 41, 59, 0.7); border-left: 4px solid #FF5252; border-radius: 0 12px 12px 0; padding: 18px 20px; margin-bottom: 24px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF5252; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 18px; font-weight: 700;\">1. About the Poet & Feminist Discourse</h3>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0 0 10px 0;\">Adrienne Cecile Rich was an influential American poet, essayist, and feminist intellectual. Throughout her prolific literary career, Rich dismantled institutionalized misogyny, compulsory heterosexuality, and the domestic suppression of women. Published in 1951 in her debut collection <i style=\"color: #FF5252;\">A Change of World</i>, this poem critiques traditional mid-twentieth-century marriage which trapped women in servitude.</p>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0;\"><b style=\"color: #FF5252;\">The Core Conflict:</b> The poem constructs a stark, aesthetic dialectic between Aunt Jennifer's crushed physical reality and her vibrant creative spirit. While her frail body is paralyzed by marital oppression, her embroidered tigers embody the uninhibited freedom, raw courage, and fierce dignity denied to her in life.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Stanza-by-Stanza Deep Analysis -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.08); border-radius: 12px; padding: 20px; margin-bottom: 24px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF5252; margin: 0 0 16px 0; font-size: 18px; font-weight: 700;\">2. Stanza-by-Stanza Critical Analysis</h3>\n    \n    <div style=\"margin-bottom: 16px; padding-bottom: 14px; border-bottom: 1px dashed rgba(255,255,255,0.1);\">\n      <h4 style=\"color: #F1F5F9; font-size: 15px; margin: 0 0 8px 0;\"><b style=\"color: #FF5252;\">Stanza 1: The Fearless Prancing Tigers on the Tapestry</b></h4>\n      <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 14px;\">The poem opens upon a vibrant needlework tapestry where Aunt Jennifer's embroidered tigers leap gracefully: <i style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">\"prance across a screen.\"</i> Rich describes them as <i style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">\"bright topaz denizens of a world of green\"</i>—their radiant golden-yellow fur glowing like precious gems against a lush jungle backdrop. Unlike their creator, these tigers inhabit a realm of absolute courage: they do not fear the human hunters or patriarchal figures standing beneath the tree. Instead, they move with aristocratic elegance and self-assured dignity: <i style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">\"they pace in sleek chivalric certainty.\"</i></p>\n    </div>\n\n    <div style=\"margin-bottom: 16px; padding-bottom: 14px; border-bottom: 1px dashed rgba(255,255,255,0.1);\">\n      <h4 style=\"color: #F1F5F9; font-size: 15px; margin: 0 0 8px 0;\"><b style=\"color: #FF5252;\">Stanza 2: The Trembling Weaver & The Burden of Matrimony</b></h4>\n      <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 14px;\">The focus abruptly shifts from the majestic creation to the pitiful creator. Aunt Jennifer's frail fingers flutter nervously through the wool, rendered so physically exhausted and psychologically shattered that she finds even the lightweight ivory needle <i style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">\"hard to pull.\"</i> Rich explains this paralysis through a crushing metaphor: <i style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">\"The massive weight of Uncle's wedding band / sits heavily upon Aunt Jennifer's hand.\"</i> The gold band, though physically featherlight, carries the immense psychic and legal weight of patriarchal subjugation, marital tyranny, and total loss of individuality.</p>\n    </div>\n\n    <div>\n      <h4 style=\"color: #F1F5F9; font-size: 15px; margin: 0 0 8px 0;\"><b style=\"color: #FF5252;\">Stanza 3: Posthumous Trauma & The Immortality of Art</b></h4>\n      <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 14px;\">Rich looks ahead to Aunt Jennifer's mortal end. Even in death, her corpse will remain physically marked by psychological trauma: <i style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">\"her terrified hands will lie / still ringed with ordeals she was mastered by.\"</i> The word 'ringed' functions as a double entendre: wearing the wedding ring and being encircled by oppressive domestic ordeals. Yet, the final rhyming couplet provides a triumphant feminist vindication: while Aunt Jennifer perishes in servitude, the tigers she conjured into existence <i style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">\"will go on prancing, proud and unafraid.\"</i> Her art transcends mortality, preserving her yearning for freedom forever.</p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Master Poetic Devices Table -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(30, 41, 59, 0.85); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.08); border-radius: 12px; padding: 20px; margin-bottom: 24px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF5252; margin: 0 0 16px 0; font-size: 18px; font-weight: 700;\">3. Master Literary & Poetic Devices Table</h3>\n    <div style=\"overflow-x: auto;\">\n      <table style=\"width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: 14px; text-align: left;\">\n        <thead>\n          <tr style=\"background: rgba(255, 82, 82, 0.15); border-bottom: 2px solid #FF5252;\">\n            <th style=\"padding: 10px 12px; color: #FF5252;\">Poetic Device</th>\n            <th style=\"padding: 10px 12px; color: #FFFFFF;\">Example from Poem</th>\n            <th style=\"padding: 10px 12px; color: #CBD5E1;\">Poetic Significance & Explanation</th>\n          </tr>\n        </thead>\n        <tbody>\n          <tr style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.06);\">\n            <td style=\"padding: 10px 12px; font-weight: bold; color: #FF5252;\">Symbolism</td>\n            <td style=\"padding: 10px 12px; color: #F1F5F9;\">\"tigers\"<br/>\"Uncle's wedding band\"<br/>\"ivory needle\"</td>\n            <td style=\"padding: 10px 12px; color: #CBD5E1;\">Tigers symbolize untamed feminine freedom and courage; the wedding band symbolizes patriarchal oppression and domestic servitude; the ivory needle symbolizes delicate artistic expression.</td>\n          </tr>\n          <tr style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.06); background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.4);\">\n            <td style=\"padding: 10px 12px; font-weight: bold; color: #FF5252;\">Pun (Double Entendre)</td>\n            <td style=\"padding: 10px 12px; color: #F1F5F9;\">\"ringed with ordeals\"</td>\n            <td style=\"padding: 10px 12px; color: #CBD5E1;\">'Ringed' simultaneously denotes wearing the physical wedding ring and being encircled, trapped, and imprisoned by oppressive domestic trials.</td>\n          </tr>\n          <tr style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.06);\">\n            <td style=\"padding: 10px 12px; font-weight: bold; color: #FF5252;\">Transferred Epithet</td>\n            <td style=\"padding: 10px 12px; color: #F1F5F9;\">\"terrified hands\"</td>\n            <td style=\"padding: 10px 12px; color: #CBD5E1;\">The hands themselves are anatomical limbs; the terror belongs to Aunt Jennifer's entire traumatized psyche, transferred to her trembling hands.</td>\n          </tr>\n          <tr style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.06); background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.4);\">\n            <td style=\"padding: 10px 12px; font-weight: bold; color: #FF5252;\">Metaphor / Hyperbole</td>\n            <td style=\"padding: 10px 12px; color: #F1F5F9;\">\"bright topaz denizens\"<br/>\"massive weight of Uncle's wedding band\"</td>\n            <td style=\"padding: 10px 12px; color: #CBD5E1;\">Tigers are compared to luminous yellow gemstones (topaz); the emotional and legal weight of marital obligations is hyperbolically described as 'massive weight'.</td>\n          </tr>\n          <tr style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.06);\">\n            <td style=\"padding: 10px 12px; font-weight: bold; color: #FF5252;\">Alliteration</td>\n            <td style=\"padding: 10px 12px; color: #F1F5F9;\">\"fingers fluttering\"<br/>\"prancing, proud\"</td>\n            <td style=\"padding: 10px 12px; color: #CBD5E1;\">Repetition of 'f' evokes trembling physical helplessness; repetition of 'p' evokes triumphant, stately majesty of the tigers.</td>\n          </tr>\n          <tr>\n            <td style=\"padding: 10px 12px; font-weight: bold; color: #FF5252;\">Irony / Antithesis</td>\n            <td style=\"padding: 10px 12px; color: #F1F5F9;\">Trembling Aunt vs. Fearless Tigers</td>\n            <td style=\"padding: 10px 12px; color: #CBD5E1;\">A frail, subjugated, terrified woman creates fearless, chivalric beasts that know no terror of men, highlighting the contrast between lived reality and artistic longing.</td>\n          </tr>\n        </tbody>\n      </table>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Vocabulary Glossary -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border-left: 4px solid #FF5252; border-radius: 0 12px 12px 0; padding: 18px 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF5252; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: 700;\">4. Essential Stanza Vocabulary</h3>\n    <div style=\"display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(220px, 1fr)); gap: 12px; font-size: 13px;\">\n      <div><b style=\"color: #FF5252;\">Prance:</b> Leap or move forward with high, springy steps.</div>\n      <div><b style=\"color: #FF5252;\">Topaz:</b> A bright, radiant yellow semi-precious gemstone.</div>\n      <div><b style=\"color: #FF5252;\">Denizens:</b> Native inhabitants or occupants of a natural habitat.</div>\n      <div><b style=\"color: #FF5252;\">Chivalric:</b> Courteous, gallant, brave, and honorable.</div>\n      <div><b style=\"color: #FF5252;\">Certainty:</b> Supreme confidence and absolute fearlessness.</div>\n      <div><b style=\"color: #FF5252;\">Fluttering:</b> Trembling or quivering with nervous weakness.</div>\n      <div><b style=\"color: #FF5252;\">Ordeals:</b> Painful, grueling, and oppressive trials.</div>\n      <div><b style=\"color: #FF5252;\">Mastered by:</b> Dominated, subjugated, and ruled over by force.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>\n";

export const c12EngF13HtmlSolutions = "\n<div style=\"padding: 16px; color: #F8FAFC; text-align: justify; font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, sans-serif; line-height: 1.85; font-size: 15px;\">\n\n  <!-- SECTION A: EXTRACT-BASED QUESTIONS -->\n  <div style=\"margin-bottom: 30px;\">\n    <div style=\"background: linear-gradient(135deg, rgba(255, 82, 82, 0.25), rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.95)); border: 1.5px solid #FF5252; border-radius: 12px; padding: 14px 18px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n      <h2 style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 20px; font-weight: 800; margin: 0 0 4px 0;\">SECTION A: Extract-Based Questions (16 Marks)</h2>\n      <p style=\"color: #FF5252; font-size: 13px; font-weight: 600; margin: 0;\">Board Pattern: 4 Objective Extracts • 4 Questions Each • Continuous Q1 to Q16</p>\n    </div>\n\n    <!-- Extract 1 -->\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(30, 41, 59, 0.7); border-left: 4px solid #FF5252; border-radius: 0 10px 10px 0; padding: 14px 18px; margin-bottom: 16px;\">\n      <p style=\"font-style: italic; color: #E2E8F0; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 14px;\">\n        \"Aunt Jennifer's tigers prance across a screen,<br/>\n        Bright topaz denizens of a world of green.<br/>\n        They do not fear the men beneath the tree;<br/>\n        They pace in sleek chivalric certainty.\"\n      </p>\n      <span style=\"color: #FF5252; font-size: 12px; font-weight: 700;\">— Extract 1 (Stanza 1)</span>\n    </div>\n\n    <!-- Q1 -->\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 82, 82, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 14px 16px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n      <h3 style=\"color: #FF5252; font-size: 15px; font-weight: 700; margin: 0 0 8px 0;\">Q1: How are Aunt Jennifer's tigers described in the opening lines?</h3>\n      <p style=\"margin: 0 0 6px 0;\"><span style=\"color: #FF5252; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> The tigers are depicted as \"bright topaz denizens of a world of green\"—vibrant golden-yellow beasts moving gracefully across an embroidered screen, inhabiting a lush jungle sanctuary with pride and dignity.</p>\n    </div>\n\n    <!-- Q2 -->\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 82, 82, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 14px 16px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n      <h3 style=\"color: #FF5252; font-size: 15px; font-weight: 700; margin: 0 0 8px 0;\">Q2: What does \"sleek chivalric certainty\" signify about the tigers?</h3>\n      <p style=\"margin: 0 0 6px 0;\"><span style=\"color: #FF5252; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> It signifies supreme confidence, poise, and noble fearlessness. The tigers move with aristocratic grace ('sleek') and honorable chivalric courage ('certainty'), unhampered by hesitation or fear of human authority.</p>\n    </div>\n\n    <!-- Q3 -->\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 82, 82, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 14px 16px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n      <h3 style=\"color: #FF5252; font-size: 15px; font-weight: 700; margin: 0 0 8px 0;\">Q3: Why do the tigers not fear the \"men beneath the tree\"?</h3>\n      <p style=\"margin: 0 0 6px 0;\"><span style=\"color: #FF5252; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> As creations of artistic imagination, the tigers are completely autonomous and wild. Unlike their creator Aunt Jennifer, who lives in terror of male dominance, the tigers are sovereign predators who disregard patriarchal authority.</p>\n    </div>\n\n    <!-- Q4 -->\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 82, 82, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 14px 16px; margin-bottom: 22px;\">\n      <h3 style=\"color: #FF5252; font-size: 15px; font-weight: 700; margin: 0 0 8px 0;\">Q4: Identify the metaphor in \"bright topaz denizens\".</h3>\n      <p style=\"margin: 0 0 6px 0;\"><span style=\"color: #FF5252; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> <b style=\"color: #FF5252;\">Metaphor</b>. The radiant, golden-yellow coats of the tigers are directly compared to the luminous brilliance of the precious gemstone 'topaz'.</p>\n    </div>\n\n    <!-- Extract 2 -->\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(30, 41, 59, 0.7); border-left: 4px solid #FF5252; border-radius: 0 10px 10px 0; padding: 14px 18px; margin-bottom: 16px;\">\n      <p style=\"font-style: italic; color: #E2E8F0; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 14px;\">\n        \"Aunt Jennifer's fingers fluttering through her wool<br/>\n        Find even the ivory needle hard to pull.<br/>\n        The massive weight of Uncle's wedding band<br/>\n        Sits heavily upon Aunt Jennifer's hand.\"\n      </p>\n      <span style=\"color: #FF5252; font-size: 12px; font-weight: 700;\">— Extract 2 (Stanza 2)</span>\n    </div>\n\n    <!-- Q5 -->\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 82, 82, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 14px 16px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n      <h3 style=\"color: #FF5252; font-size: 15px; font-weight: 700; margin: 0 0 8px 0;\">Q5: Why are Aunt Jennifer's fingers \"fluttering\" through the wool?</h3>\n      <p style=\"margin: 0 0 6px 0;\"><span style=\"color: #FF5252; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> Her fingers flutter because she is terrified, nervous, and physically exhausted. Years of chronic emotional abuse, domestic subjugation, and patriarchal tyranny have shattered her nervous system.</p>\n    </div>\n\n    <!-- Q6 -->\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 82, 82, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 14px 16px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n      <h3 style=\"color: #FF5252; font-size: 15px; font-weight: 700; margin: 0 0 8px 0;\">Q6: Why does Aunt Jennifer find the lightweight ivory needle hard to pull?</h3>\n      <p style=\"margin: 0 0 6px 0;\"><span style=\"color: #FF5252; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> An ivory needle weighs almost nothing, but to Aunt Jennifer it feels unbearably heavy because she is drained of physical vitality and morale by marital oppression, making even simple artistic tasks a struggle.</p>\n    </div>\n\n    <!-- Q7 -->\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 82, 82, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 14px 16px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n      <h3 style=\"color: #FF5252; font-size: 15px; font-weight: 700; margin: 0 0 8px 0;\">Q7: What does the \"massive weight of Uncle's wedding band\" symbolize?</h3>\n      <p style=\"margin: 0 0 6px 0;\"><span style=\"color: #FF5252; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> It symbolizes the crushing legal, emotional, and social bondage of patriarchal marriage. It represents Uncle's absolute ownership over her, turning a symbol of matrimonial union into shackles of servitude.</p>\n    </div>\n\n    <!-- Q8 -->\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 82, 82, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 14px 16px; margin-bottom: 22px;\">\n      <h3 style=\"color: #FF5252; font-size: 15px; font-weight: 700; margin: 0 0 8px 0;\">Q8: Identify the poetic device in \"fingers fluttering\".</h3>\n      <p style=\"margin: 0 0 6px 0;\"><span style=\"color: #FF5252; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> <b style=\"color: #FF5252;\">Alliteration</b>. The repetition of the soft 'f' sound acoustically mirrors the trembling, fragile fluttering of her weary fingers.</p>\n    </div>\n\n    <!-- Extract 3 -->\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(30, 41, 59, 0.7); border-left: 4px solid #FF5252; border-radius: 0 10px 10px 0; padding: 14px 18px; margin-bottom: 16px;\">\n      <p style=\"font-style: italic; color: #E2E8F0; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 14px;\">\n        \"When Aunt is dead, her terrified hands will lie<br/>\n        Still ringed with ordeals she was mastered by.<br/>\n        The tigers in the panel that she made<br/>\n        Will go on prancing, proud and unafraid.\"\n      </p>\n      <span style=\"color: #FF5252; font-size: 12px; font-weight: 700;\">— Extract 3 (Stanza 3)</span>\n    </div>\n\n    <!-- Q9 -->\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 82, 82, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 14px 16px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n      <h3 style=\"color: #FF5252; font-size: 15px; font-weight: 700; margin: 0 0 8px 0;\">Q9: What is meant by the transferred epithet \"terrified hands\"?</h3>\n      <p style=\"margin: 0 0 6px 0;\"><span style=\"color: #FF5252; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> The terror is an emotional state belonging to Aunt Jennifer's entire traumatized psyche, but it is transferred grammatically to her 'hands', highlighting how deeply fear has soaked into her physical body.</p>\n    </div>\n\n    <!-- Q10 -->\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 82, 82, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 14px 16px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n      <h3 style=\"color: #FF5252; font-size: 15px; font-weight: 700; margin: 0 0 8px 0;\">Q10: Explain the double meaning (pun) of the word \"ringed\".</h3>\n      <p style=\"margin: 0 0 6px 0;\"><span style=\"color: #FF5252; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> 'Ringed' literally refers to wearing the gold wedding ring on her finger. Metaphorically, it means being encircled, trapped, and imprisoned by the grueling ordeals and demands of patriarchal marriage.</p>\n    </div>\n\n    <!-- Q11 -->\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 82, 82, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 14px 16px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n      <h3 style=\"color: #FF5252; font-size: 15px; font-weight: 700; margin: 0 0 8px 0;\">Q11: How do the tigers behave after Aunt Jennifer's death?</h3>\n      <p style=\"margin: 0 0 6px 0;\"><span style=\"color: #FF5252; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> The embroidered tigers will continue \"prancing, proud and unafraid\". Immortalized in art, they outlive their creator and her oppressor, standing as an undying monument to courage and freedom.</p>\n    </div>\n\n    <!-- Q12 -->\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 82, 82, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 14px 16px; margin-bottom: 22px;\">\n      <h3 style=\"color: #FF5252; font-size: 15px; font-weight: 700; margin: 0 0 8px 0;\">Q12: What stark irony is presented in the final two lines?</h3>\n      <p style=\"margin: 0 0 6px 0;\"><span style=\"color: #FF5252; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> The irony is that the frail, subjugated woman who lived and died in perpetual terror has created beasts of immortal courage and fearless pride that will never know subjugation.</p>\n    </div>\n\n    <!-- Extract 4 (Comprehensive Synthesis Extract) -->\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(30, 41, 59, 0.7); border-left: 4px solid #FF5252; border-radius: 0 10px 10px 0; padding: 14px 18px; margin-bottom: 16px;\">\n      <p style=\"font-style: italic; color: #E2E8F0; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-size: 14px;\">\n        \"They pace in sleek chivalric certainty.<br/>\n        ...<br/>\n        Find even the ivory needle hard to pull.<br/>\n        ...<br/>\n        Will go on prancing, proud and unafraid.\"\n      </p>\n      <span style=\"color: #FF5252; font-size: 12px; font-weight: 700;\">— Extract 4 (Thematic Synthesis)</span>\n    </div>\n\n    <!-- Q13 -->\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 82, 82, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 14px 16px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n      <h3 style=\"color: #FF5252; font-size: 15px; font-weight: 700; margin: 0 0 8px 0;\">Q13: How does the poem demonstrate the therapeutic value of art for suppressed women?</h3>\n      <p style=\"margin: 0 0 6px 0;\"><span style=\"color: #FF5252; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> Needlework provides an emotional sanctuary for Aunt Jennifer. Denied agency and speech in marriage, she expresses her repressed desire for freedom, defiance, and dignity through the fierce tigers she stitches onto the tapestry.</p>\n    </div>\n\n    <!-- Q14 -->\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 82, 82, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 14px 16px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n      <h3 style=\"color: #FF5252; font-size: 15px; font-weight: 700; margin: 0 0 8px 0;\">Q14: What is the tone of the poet toward Aunt Jennifer?</h3>\n      <p style=\"margin: 0 0 6px 0;\"><span style=\"color: #FF5252; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> The poet's tone is deeply compassionate, empathetic, and politically indignant, lamenting the systemic destruction of a woman's vitality while revering her artistic resilience.</p>\n    </div>\n\n    <!-- Q15 -->\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 82, 82, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 14px 16px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n      <h3 style=\"color: #FF5252; font-size: 15px; font-weight: 700; margin: 0 0 8px 0;\">Q15: Identify alliteration in \"prancing, proud\".</h3>\n      <p style=\"margin: 0 0 6px 0;\"><span style=\"color: #FF5252; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> <b style=\"color: #FF5252;\">Alliteration</b>. The plosive 'p' sound reinforces the bold, stately, and unyielding majesty of the tigers.</p>\n    </div>\n\n    <!-- Q16 -->\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 82, 82, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 14px 16px; margin-bottom: 22px;\">\n      <h3 style=\"color: #FF5252; font-size: 15px; font-weight: 700; margin: 0 0 8px 0;\">Q16: How does the contrast between creator and creation define the poem?</h3>\n      <p style=\"margin: 0 0 6px 0;\"><span style=\"color: #FF5252; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> Aunt Jennifer is timid, oppressed, and physically bounded by marital subjugation; her tigers are bold, majestic, and immortal. This contrast emphasizes that the human spirit's yearning for freedom cannot be extinguished by oppression.</p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION B: SHORT ANSWER QUESTIONS -->\n  <div style=\"margin-bottom: 30px;\">\n    <div style=\"background: linear-gradient(135deg, rgba(255, 82, 82, 0.25), rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.95)); border: 1.5px solid #FF5252; border-radius: 12px; padding: 14px 18px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n      <h2 style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 20px; font-weight: 800; margin: 0 0 4px 0;\">SECTION B: Short Answer Questions (3 Marks • 40–50 Words)</h2>\n      <p style=\"color: #FF5252; font-size: 13px; font-weight: 600; margin: 0;\">Board Standard: Feminist Analysis & Structural Reasoning • 12 Questions (Q17 to Q28)</p>\n    </div>\n\n    <!-- Q17 -->\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border-left: 3px solid #FF5252; border-radius: 0 10px 10px 0; padding: 14px 16px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n      <h3 style=\"color: #FF5252; font-size: 15px; font-weight: 700; margin: 0 0 8px 0;\">Q17: How do Aunt Jennifer's tigers differ from real, biological tigers?</h3>\n      <p style=\"margin: 0 0 6px 0;\"><span style=\"color: #FF5252; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> Real tigers are flesh-and-blood animals inhabiting jungles, vulnerable to hunting and death. Aunt Jennifer's tigers are idealized artistic symbols stitched on wool—immortal, chivalric beasts embodying absolute freedom and fearlessness.</p>\n    </div>\n\n    <!-- Q18 -->\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border-left: 3px solid #FF5252; border-radius: 0 10px 10px 0; padding: 14px 16px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n      <h3 style=\"color: #FF5252; font-size: 15px; font-weight: 700; margin: 0 0 8px 0;\">Q18: Why is Uncle's wedding band described as sitting \"heavily\" upon her hand?</h3>\n      <p style=\"margin: 0 0 6px 0;\"><span style=\"color: #FF5252; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> The wedding ring is physically small and light, but it carries the heavy psychic burden of patriarchal ownership. It symbolizes decades of forced domestic compliance, loss of personal liberty, and emotional oppression.</p>\n    </div>\n\n    <!-- Q19 -->\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border-left: 3px solid #FF5252; border-radius: 0 10px 10px 0; padding: 14px 16px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n      <h3 style=\"color: #FF5252; font-size: 15px; font-weight: 700; margin: 0 0 8px 0;\">Q19: What are the \"ordeals\" Aunt Jennifer was mastered by?</h3>\n      <p style=\"margin: 0 0 6px 0;\"><span style=\"color: #FF5252; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> The ordeals refer to the relentless, suffocating demands of a patriarchal marital household—suppression of her voice, fear of Uncle's temper, loss of financial independence, and forced servitude to traditional domestic expectations.</p>\n    </div>\n\n    <!-- Q20 -->\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border-left: 3px solid #FF5252; border-radius: 0 10px 10px 0; padding: 14px 16px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n      <h3 style=\"color: #FF5252; font-size: 15px; font-weight: 700; margin: 0 0 8px 0;\">Q20: Why did Aunt Jennifer choose to embroider tigers specifically?</h3>\n      <p style=\"margin: 0 0 6px 0;\"><span style=\"color: #FF5252; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> Tigers represent the antithesis of her lived reality. By stitching powerful, confident, and fearless apex predators, Aunt Jennifer channeled her repressed yearnings for personal strength, self-assertion, and bodily autonomy.</p>\n    </div>\n\n    <!-- Q21 -->\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border-left: 3px solid #FF5252; border-radius: 0 10px 10px 0; padding: 14px 16px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n      <h3 style=\"color: #FF5252; font-size: 15px; font-weight: 700; margin: 0 0 8px 0;\">Q21: How does Adrienne Rich use the word \"mastered\" in the third stanza?</h3>\n      <p style=\"margin: 0 0 6px 0;\"><span style=\"color: #FF5252; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> \"Mastered by\" emphasizes total subjugation. Aunt Jennifer was ruled over like an indentured servant by her husband and patriarchal societal codes, unable to exercise self-determination throughout her life.</p>\n    </div>\n\n    <!-- Q22 -->\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border-left: 3px solid #FF5252; border-radius: 0 10px 10px 0; padding: 14px 16px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n      <h3 style=\"color: #FF5252; font-size: 15px; font-weight: 700; margin: 0 0 8px 0;\">Q22: Why will her hands remain \"terrified\" even when she is dead?</h3>\n      <p style=\"margin: 0 0 6px 0;\"><span style=\"color: #FF5252; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> Trauma has permanently shaped her bodily memory. Decades of subservience and terror have become ingrained in her physical being, leaving their mark even on her lifeless corpse.</p>\n    </div>\n\n    <!-- Q23 -->\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border-left: 3px solid #FF5252; border-radius: 0 10px 10px 0; padding: 14px 16px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n      <h3 style=\"color: #FF5252; font-size: 15px; font-weight: 700; margin: 0 0 8px 0;\">Q23: How does the poem illustrate the immortality of art?</h3>\n      <p style=\"margin: 0 0 6px 0;\"><span style=\"color: #FF5252; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> Aunt Jennifer perishes in physical subjugation, but the embroidered tapestry survives. Long after she and Uncle turn to dust, her vibrant, fearless tigers will continue to prance across the screen, immortalizing her spirit.</p>\n    </div>\n\n    <!-- Q24 -->\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border-left: 3px solid #FF5252; border-radius: 0 10px 10px 0; padding: 14px 16px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n      <h3 style=\"color: #FF5252; font-size: 15px; font-weight: 700; margin: 0 0 8px 0;\">Q24: What is the significance of the \"ivory needle\" in the second stanza?</h3>\n      <p style=\"margin: 0 0 6px 0;\"><span style=\"color: #FF5252; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> Ivory is an expensive, smooth, and lightweight material. The fact that Aunt Jennifer finds even this featherlight needle burdensome demonstrates that her exhaustion is psychological rather than muscular.</p>\n    </div>\n\n    <!-- Q25 -->\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border-left: 3px solid #FF5252; border-radius: 0 10px 10px 0; padding: 14px 16px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n      <h3 style=\"color: #FF5252; font-size: 15px; font-weight: 700; margin: 0 0 8px 0;\">Q25: What picture of Uncle emerges in the poem, though he never speaks?</h3>\n      <p style=\"margin: 0 0 6px 0;\"><span style=\"color: #FF5252; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> Uncle is portrayed as an overbearing, authoritarian patriarch. Though absent from the scene, his presence looms through the oppressive \"wedding band\" that controls and exhausts Aunt Jennifer.</p>\n    </div>\n\n    <!-- Q26 -->\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border-left: 3px solid #FF5252; border-radius: 0 10px 10px 0; padding: 14px 16px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n      <h3 style=\"color: #FF5252; font-size: 15px; font-weight: 700; margin: 0 0 8px 0;\">Q26: Why does Adrienne Rich use the word \"prance\" to describe the tigers' movement?</h3>\n      <p style=\"margin: 0 0 6px 0;\"><span style=\"color: #FF5252; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> \"Prance\" connotes proud, buoyant, and celebratory movement. It stands in direct opposition to Aunt Jennifer's timid, restricted, and nervous gestures, accentuating the tigers' untamed vitality.</p>\n    </div>\n\n    <!-- Q27 -->\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border-left: 3px solid #FF5252; border-radius: 0 10px 10px 0; padding: 14px 16px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n      <h3 style=\"color: #FF5252; font-size: 15px; font-weight: 700; margin: 0 0 8px 0;\">Q27: How does the rhyme scheme reinforce the poem's themes?</h3>\n      <p style=\"margin: 0 0 6px 0;\"><span style=\"color: #FF5252; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> Composed in neat rhyming couplets (AABB CCDD EEFF), the rigid, formal rhyme mirrors the strict societal confinement governing Aunt Jennifer's life, while the fiery content expresses her rebellion.</p>\n    </div>\n\n    <!-- Q28 -->\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border-left: 3px solid #FF5252; border-radius: 0 10px 10px 0; padding: 14px 16px; margin-bottom: 22px;\">\n      <h3 style=\"color: #FF5252; font-size: 15px; font-weight: 700; margin: 0 0 8px 0;\">Q28: What feminist message does Adrienne Rich communicate through Aunt Jennifer's fate?</h3>\n      <p style=\"margin: 0 0 6px 0;\"><span style=\"color: #FF5252; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> Rich exposes how institutionalized marriage subjugates women's individuality. She calls for dismantling patriarchal control so that women can live fearlessly in reality, not merely through embroidery.</p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION C: LONG ANSWER QUESTIONS -->\n  <div style=\"margin-bottom: 30px;\">\n    <div style=\"background: linear-gradient(135deg, rgba(255, 82, 82, 0.25), rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.95)); border: 1.5px solid #FF5252; border-radius: 12px; padding: 14px 18px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n      <h2 style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 20px; font-weight: 800; margin: 0 0 4px 0;\">SECTION C: Long Analytical Questions (6 Marks • 120–150 Words)</h2>\n      <p style=\"color: #FF5252; font-size: 13px; font-weight: 600; margin: 0;\">3-Tier Model: Feminist Framework • Symbolic Dialectic • Board Resolution • Q29 to Q34</p>\n    </div>\n\n    <!-- Q29 -->\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 82, 82, 0.3); border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n      <h3 style=\"color: #FF5252; font-size: 16px; font-weight: 700; margin: 0 0 10px 0;\">Q29: Analyze the sharp contrast between Aunt Jennifer and her embroidered tigers as an indictment of patriarchal marriage.</h3>\n      <p style=\"margin: 0 0 8px 0;\"><span style=\"color: #FF5252; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span></p>\n      <p style=\"margin: 0 0 8px 0;\"><b style=\"color: #FF5252;\">1. The Oppressed Creator:</b> Adrienne Rich establishes an unmistakable dialectic between Aunt Jennifer and her art. In her domestic life, Aunt Jennifer is nervous, frail, and thoroughly broken. Her fingers \"flutter\" through the wool, finding even a lightweight ivory needle burdensome. She is weighed down by \"Uncle's wedding band\"—a symbol of patriarchal dominance that has reduced her to an anxious, subjugated shadow.</p>\n      <p style=\"margin: 0 0 8px 0;\"><b style=\"color: #FF5252;\">2. The Fearless Creation:</b> In contrast, the tigers she stitches onto the tapestry are \"bright topaz denizens\" roaming a vibrant green jungle. They pace with \"sleek chivalric certainty\" and have no fear of the men standing beneath the trees. They embody everything Aunt Jennifer is denied: sovereignty, strength, and proud independence.</p>\n      <p style=\"margin: 0;\"><b style=\"color: #FF5252;\">3. Critical Conclusion:</b> This stark contrast illustrates how patriarchal marriage stifles women's spirits. Denied freedom in life, Aunt Jennifer projects her unquenchable desire for autonomy onto her needlework, leaving behind an immortal critique of male dominance.</p>\n    </div>\n\n    <!-- Q30 -->\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 82, 82, 0.3); border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n      <h3 style=\"color: #FF5252; font-size: 16px; font-weight: 700; margin: 0 0 10px 0;\">Q30: Examine the symbolic significance of \"Uncle's wedding band\", the \"ivory needle\", and the \"screen\".</h3>\n      <p style=\"margin: 0 0 8px 0;\"><span style=\"color: #FF5252; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span></p>\n      <p style=\"margin: 0 0 8px 0;\"><b style=\"color: #FF5252;\">1. The Wedding Band as Shackle:</b> The wedding band is described not as a symbol of love, but as having a \"massive weight\" that sits heavily upon her hand. It belongs to \"Uncle\", signifying legal and social ownership. It symbolizes institutionalized marital subjugation, reminding Aunt Jennifer of her loss of liberty and identity.</p>\n      <p style=\"margin: 0 0 8px 0;\"><b style=\"color: #FF5252;\">2. The Ivory Needle as Creative Struggle:</b> The ivory needle represents artistic expression and feminine craft. Aunt Jennifer's inability to pull this lightweight tool highlights how domestic intimidation has drained her vitality, making creative self-expression an uphill battle.</p>\n      <p style=\"margin: 0;\"><b style=\"color: #FF5252;\">3. The Screen as a Canvas of Rebellion:</b> The tapestry screen serves as her sanctuary and canvas of rebellion. Constrained within domestic walls, she uses the screen to construct an alternative reality of golden tigers, asserting her suppressed yearning for freedom.</p>\n    </div>\n\n    <!-- Q31 -->\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 82, 82, 0.3); border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n      <h3 style=\"color: #FF5252; font-size: 16px; font-weight: 700; margin: 0 0 10px 0;\">Q31: Discuss the final stanza of the poem as an exploration of posthumous trauma and the transcendence of art.</h3>\n      <p style=\"margin: 0 0 8px 0;\"><span style=\"color: #FF5252; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span></p>\n      <p style=\"margin: 0 0 8px 0;\"><b style=\"color: #FF5252;\">1. The Permanence of Trauma:</b> In the final stanza, Rich envisions Aunt Jennifer's death. Even in her coffin, her \"terrified hands\" will remain \"ringed with ordeals she was mastered by.\" The ring endures beyond mortality, showing that the emotional damage inflicted by domestic subjugation leaves permanent scars on the human soul.</p>\n      <p style=\"margin: 0 0 8px 0;\"><b style=\"color: #FF5252;\">2. The Triumph of Artistic Creation:</b> However, the closing couplet shifts from tragedy to triumph: <i>\"The tigers in the panel that she made / Will go on prancing, proud and unafraid.\"</i> While the mortal woman perishes, her art endures, outliving both her and her oppressor.</p>\n      <p style=\"margin: 0;\"><b style=\"color: #FF5252;\">3. Critical Conclusion:</b> Rich highlights the paradox of female survival under patriarchy: though women's bodies may be mastered and broken, their creative spirit can produce enduring expressions of freedom that inspire future generations.</p>\n    </div>\n\n    <!-- Q32 -->\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 82, 82, 0.3); border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n      <h3 style=\"color: #FF5252; font-size: 16px; font-weight: 700; margin: 0 0 10px 0;\">Q32: Analyze Adrienne Rich's use of poetic devices: transferred epithets, puns, and imagery.</h3>\n      <p style=\"margin: 0 0 8px 0;\"><span style=\"color: #FF5252; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span></p>\n      <p style=\"margin: 0 0 8px 0;\"><b style=\"color: #FF5252;\">1. Imagery & Color Contrast:</b> Rich uses vivid visual imagery to establish emotional resonance. The \"bright topaz\" yellow of the tigers flashes against the \"world of green\", conveying energy and life. This contrasts sharply with the somber domestic image of trembling fingers struggling with a needle under the shadow of Uncle's wedding band.</p>\n      <p style=\"margin: 0 0 8px 0;\"><b style=\"color: #FF5252;\">2. Transferred Epithet & Pun:</b> The phrase \"terrified hands\" transfers the psychological terror of Aunt Jennifer's mind to her hands. Furthermore, the word \"ringed\" functions as a layered pun: it refers to the physical wedding band and to being trapped by domestic ordeals.</p>\n      <p style=\"margin: 0;\"><b style=\"color: #FF5252;\">3. Critical Conclusion:</b> These literary devices work together to heighten the emotional tension between submission and defiance, giving the short poem its memorable power.</p>\n    </div>\n\n    <!-- Q33 -->\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 82, 82, 0.3); border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n      <h3 style=\"color: #FF5252; font-size: 16px; font-weight: 700; margin: 0 0 10px 0;\">Q33: How does Adrienne Rich deconstruct traditional twentieth-century gender roles in \"Aunt Jennifer's Tigers\"?</h3>\n      <p style=\"margin: 0 0 8px 0;\"><span style=\"color: #FF5252; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span></p>\n      <p style=\"margin: 0 0 8px 0;\"><b style=\"color: #FF5252;\">1. Subverting the Ideology of Domestic Bliss:</b> Mid-twentieth-century society promoted marriage and domesticity as the ultimate fulfillment for women. Rich dismantles this myth by portraying marriage as an oppressive institution (\"ordeals she was mastered by\") that drains women of vitality and reduces them to nervous wrecks.</p>\n      <p style=\"margin: 0 0 8px 0;\"><b style=\"color: #FF5252;\">2. Reclamation of Power through Craft:</b> Traditional feminine crafts like embroidery, often dismissed as frivolous domestic hobbies, are repurposed as tools of quiet defiance. Through needlework, Aunt Jennifer creates fierce apex predators that ignore male hunters, reclaiming agency in a world that denies it to her.</p>\n      <p style=\"margin: 0;\"><b style=\"color: #FF5252;\">3. Critical Conclusion:</b> Rich demonstrates that patriarchal dominance is unnatural and oppressive, validating women's hidden rebellion and calling for a world where women can live with the same fearless dignity as the tigers on the screen.</p>\n    </div>\n\n    <!-- Q34 -->\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 82, 82, 0.3); border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px; margin-bottom: 22px;\">\n      <h3 style=\"color: #FF5252; font-size: 16px; font-weight: 700; margin: 0 0 10px 0;\">Q34: Evaluate \"Aunt Jennifer's Tigers\" as a feminist manifesto on female agency, oppression, and legacy.</h3>\n      <p style=\"margin: 0 0 8px 0;\"><span style=\"color: #FF5252; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span></p>\n      <p style=\"margin: 0 0 8px 0;\"><b style=\"color: #FF5252;\">1. The Reality of Oppression:</b> The poem serves as a powerful critique of patriarchal domination. Aunt Jennifer's trembling hands and heavy wedding ring symbolize the systemic subordination experienced by countless women whose voices and identities were erased within traditional marriages.</p>\n      <p style=\"margin: 0 0 8px 0;\"><b style=\"color: #FF5252;\">2. Agency through Artistic Expression:</b> Despite severe constraints, Aunt Jennifer refuses total spiritual defeat. By creating fearless tigers pacing in chivalric certainty, she exercises agency in the only domain open to her, using her art as a vehicle for self-affirmation.</p>\n      <p style=\"margin: 0;\"><b style=\"color: #FF5252;\">3. An Enduring Legacy:</b> Her legacy is twofold: a cautionary portrait of a woman broken by domestic oppression, and an inspiring testament to the power of art. The prancing tigers survive as a lasting symbol of defiance, calling upon future generations of women to claim their freedom in life, not just in art.</p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION D: NCERT TEXTBOOK EXERCISES -->\n  <div style=\"margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <div style=\"background: linear-gradient(135deg, rgba(255, 82, 82, 0.25), rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.95)); border: 1.5px solid #FF5252; border-radius: 12px; padding: 14px 18px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n      <h2 style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 20px; font-weight: 800; margin: 0 0 4px 0;\">SECTION D: NCERT Textbook Exercises (Thinking About The Poem)</h2>\n      <p style=\"color: #FF5252; font-size: 13px; font-weight: 600; margin: 0;\">Official Curriculum Solutions • Questions Q35 to Q38</p>\n    </div>\n\n    <!-- Q35 -->\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 82, 82, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 14px 16px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n      <h3 style=\"color: #FF5252; font-size: 15px; font-weight: 700; margin: 0 0 8px 0;\">Q35: How do 'denizens' and 'chivalric' add to our understanding of the tiger's attitudes?</h3>\n      <p style=\"margin: 0 0 6px 0;\"><span style=\"color: #FF5252; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> 'Denizens' shows that the tigers are natural masters of their jungle domain, completely at home and sovereign in their habitat. 'Chivalric' portrays them as noble, gallant, and confident knights. Together, these terms highlight their supreme poise, dignity, and fearlessness, in sharp contrast to Aunt Jennifer's anxious domestic reality.</p>\n    </div>\n\n    <!-- Q36 -->\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 82, 82, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 14px 16px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n      <h3 style=\"color: #FF5252; font-size: 15px; font-weight: 700; margin: 0 0 8px 0;\">Q36: Why do you think Aunt Jennifer's hands are 'fluttering through her wool' in the second stanza? Why is she finding the needle so hard to pull?</h3>\n      <p style=\"margin: 0 0 6px 0;\"><span style=\"color: #FF5252; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> Aunt Jennifer's hands flutter because she is terrified, nervous, and emotionally exhausted from years of domestic intimidation and marital tyranny. She finds even the lightweight ivory needle hard to pull because her exhaustion is psychological—the crushing weight of Uncle's dominance drains her physical vitality and spirit.</p>\n    </div>\n\n    <!-- Q37 -->\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 82, 82, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 14px 16px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n      <h3 style=\"color: #FF5252; font-size: 15px; font-weight: 700; margin: 0 0 8px 0;\">Q37: What is suggested by the image 'massive weight of Uncle's wedding band'?</h3>\n      <p style=\"margin: 0 0 6px 0;\"><span style=\"color: #FF5252; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> It suggests the crushing burden of patriarchal marriage. The gold ring is physically small and light, but emotionally it represents decades of forced obedience, loss of personal freedom, and Uncle's authoritarian ownership, turning her married life into a state of perpetual subjugation.</p>\n    </div>\n\n    <!-- Q38 -->\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 82, 82, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 14px 16px; margin-bottom: 14px;\">\n      <h3 style=\"color: #FF5252; font-size: 15px; font-weight: 700; margin: 0 0 8px 0;\">Q38: Of what or of whom is Aunt Jennifer terrified with in the third stanza?</h3>\n      <p style=\"margin: 0 0 6px 0;\"><span style=\"color: #FF5252; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> Aunt Jennifer is terrified of her domineering husband (Uncle) and the oppressive patriarchal household system that governed her existence. Even in death, her hands reflect this trauma, remaining scarred by the trials and domestic subjugation that controlled her life.</p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n</div>\n";

